WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:05.050
There's plenty of horror movies that start
slowly and methodically, letting viewers settle

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:07.660
into a rhythm before the terror really comes.

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:09.770
These are not those movies.

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:13.000
Here are the scariest opening scenes in horror
movies.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.380
Friday the 13th Part V: A New Beginning

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:20.390
Other Friday the 13th sequels leaned hard
into funhouse territory, relying just as much

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:25.630
on morbid humor as gory shocks - but not in
the case of Friday the 13th Part V: A New

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:26.669
Beginning.

00:00:26.669 --> 00:00:30.919
Corey Feldman reprises his role as a young
Tommy Jarvis in the dream sequence that opens

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:31.919
the film.

00:00:31.919 --> 00:00:37.410
On a dark, stormy night, Tommy finds himself
observing Jason's grave as two would-be grave

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.520
robbers are digging it up.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:47.559
[Thunder and ominous music]

00:00:47.559 --> 00:00:52.010
It doesn't go well, and Feldman's reactions
as he witnesses Jason rising once again to

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.160
butcher the hapless crooks, sell Tommy's sheer
panic and terror perfectly.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.559
[Thunder and ominous music]

00:01:03.559 --> 00:01:07.360
The brilliant opening is arguably the best
of the series.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:08.870
Twilight Zone: The Movie

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:15.650
1983's Twilight Zone film was largely short
of true scares, but its brief, pre-credits

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:20.160
opening sequence has added a phrase to the
pop culture lexicon that still has the power

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.410
to make our skin crawl:

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.680
"Hey!

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.490
You wanna see something really scary?"

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:27.040
"You bet."

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.040
"Really?"

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.040
"Yeah."

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.680
"Okay, this is really, really scary now."

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.170
Comedians Dan Aykroyd and Albert Brooks put
the audience at ease as motorists on a lonely

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:37.450
road at night.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.290
But when Brooks tries to rattle Aykroyd by
turning off his headlights, Aykroyd ups the

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:46.850
ante by insisting that Brooks pull over so
that he can show him something scarier - and

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.980
boy, does he ever.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:51.400
"What are you doing?"

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.360
[Scary monster noises]

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.840
Scream

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:03.790
Wes Craven's 1996 deconstruction of the horror
genre, Scream started a trend of self-awareness

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.170
in fright flicks that continues to this day.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:10.780
But it's easy to forget what a virtuoso piece
of filmmaking its opening sequence is.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.250
Drew Barrymore appears as a young woman named
Casey, who receives what she thinks is a prank

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:16.250
phone call.

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.250
"Hello?"

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.440
"Why don't you want to talk to me?"

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.550
"Who is this?"

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:23.880
"You tell me your name, I'll tell you mine."

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:25.360
"I don't think so."

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.420
As the guy quizzes her about horror movies,
the tone of the call goes from playful to

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:34.060
sinister to outright terrifying as Casey slowly
realizes that this is no joke.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.190
"You never told me your name."

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.320
"Why do you want to know my name?"

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.660
"Cuz I wanna know who I'm looking at."

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.330
"What did you say?"

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:48.920
The scene is an absolute master class in playing
on audience expectations and building tension.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.430
Scream's influence has towered over horror
for the last couple of decades, but it may

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:57.010
not have lingered quite as long in the popular
imagination without this opening.

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:58.010
Jaws

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.060
Virtually everything that could have gone
wrong with Jaws did, up to and including the

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:04.880
mechanical sharks not working up to expectations.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.100
But the technical problems ended up enhancing
the film in ways nobody could have foreseen,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:14.020
as Stephen Spielberg wisely chose to keep
his toothy menace offscreen whenever possible.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:18.340
The opening scene shows just how effective
this technique is as a young swimmer named

00:03:18.340 --> 00:03:23.090
Chrissy goes for the last moonlit skinny dip
she'll ever take.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:27.920
[Gasping for breath]

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.410
For the actual attack, Spielberg and his crew
employed an underwater system of ropes to

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:37.680
jerk the actress back and forth, and all we
can see are her terrified reactions, which

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.520
she completely sells.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:45.950
[Screaming]

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:49.960
Jaws is a film that set the template for the
summer tentpole picture and has been ripped

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:54.710
off more times than can be counted - but its
opening retains all of its power over four

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:56.610
decades later.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.610
Drag Me to Hell

00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:03.200
Sam Raimi made his triumphant return to gonzo
horror with 2009's Drag Me to Hell, the story

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.580
of a loan officer named Christine who runs
afoul of a curse.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:10.950
Unless she can find a way to stop it, Christine
will be tormented by a demon for three days

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.920
before getting dragged to hell.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:17.370
The opening scene makes it clear that it's
definitely not just in Christine's head, either.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:21.559
A young boy is brought to a medium because
he's been hearing voices after he stole a

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:22.680
necklace from an old woman.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:37.160
All of the old, Evil Dead-esque Raimi calling
cards make their return: the off-kilter angles,

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:41.160
the eye-popping closeups, the crazily kinetic
camera work.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:46.000
[Screaming]

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.880
[Thud]

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:55.320
But the scene also establishes that the demon
has no mercy, as the boy's frantic cries for

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.120
help don't stop his fate.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:05.640
[Screaming]

